# Next.js App Router Rules

## Core Framework Rules

- Use App Router only.
- Never use Pages Router.
- Never use `getServerSideProps`.
- Never use `getStaticProps`.
- Never use legacy routing patterns.

The platform is fully App Router-based.

---

## Rendering Philosophy

The platform is:

```txt
Server-first
SEO-first
Performance-first
```

Prefer:
- Server Components
- static rendering
- ISR
- minimal hydration

Avoid:
- unnecessary client rendering
- hydration-heavy pages
- browser-dependent SEO content

---

## Server Component Rules

Use Server Components by default.

Server Components should handle:
- SEO content
- metadata
- schema rendering
- content rendering
- route composition
- data preparation

Do not add `"use client"` unless interaction requires it.

---

## Client Component Rules

Client Components are allowed only for:

- modals
- menus
- filters
- carousels
- browser APIs
- CTA interactions
- lightweight animation interaction

Keep Client Components:
- isolated
- small
- intentional

Avoid:
- client-rendering entire pages
- unnecessary hydration
- client-side SEO rendering

---

## Layout Rules

Use nested layouts intentionally.

Recommended:

```txt
app/
  layout.tsx
  page.tsx

  tattoo-blog/
    layout.tsx
    page.tsx
    [slug]/
      page.tsx
```

Layouts should handle:
- shared structure
- metadata wrappers
- route-level consistency

Avoid:
- duplicated wrappers across pages

---

## Metadata Rules

Use App Router metadata APIs.

Use:
- `generateMetadata`
- centralized metadata helpers

Do not manually duplicate metadata logic.

Metadata should support:
- title
- description
- Open Graph
- canonical URLs
- region awareness

---

## Metadata Philosophy

Metadata must remain:

- typed
- centralized
- SEO-safe
- region-aware

Avoid:
- inline duplicated metadata
- hardcoded region titles
- inconsistent OG generation

---

## Static Generation Rules

Prefer:
- static rendering
- `generateStaticParams`
- ISR

Use ISR for:
- blog content
- gallery systems
- review systems
- dynamic SEO content

Avoid unnecessary runtime rendering.

---

## Route Handler Rules

Use Route Handlers for:

- webhooks
- review sync endpoints
- external callbacks
- internal APIs
- health endpoints

Preferred structure:

```txt
app/api/.../route.ts
```

Avoid:
- mixing API logic inside pages
- client-side secret handling

---

## Server Action Rules

Use Server Actions for:

- CTA submissions
- lightweight mutations
- Airtable writes
- contact flows

Keep Server Actions:
- small
- focused
- secure

Avoid:
- bloated action logic
- large business orchestration inside actions

---

## Loading State Rules

Use route-level loading states intentionally.

Loading states should feel:
- lightweight
- premium
- fast

Avoid:
- excessive skeleton choreography
- playful loaders
- layout shifting

---

## Error Boundary Rules

Use route-level error handling where meaningful.

Error experiences should:
- remain stable
- preserve UX clarity
- avoid breaking layouts

Avoid:
- exposing raw errors
- crashing entire route trees

---

## Suspense Rules

Use Suspense carefully.

Good use cases:
- deferred media
- non-critical sections
- async supporting systems

Avoid:
- suspense-wrapping entire SEO pages unnecessarily

---

## Caching Philosophy

Prefer:
- stable caching
- ISR
- predictable invalidation

Avoid:
- excessive no-store rendering
- unstable runtime fetching
- unnecessary cache busting

---

## Dynamic Rendering Rules

Use dynamic rendering only when truly required.

Avoid:
- defaulting everything to dynamic
- runtime-heavy route generation

The platform should remain:
```txt
fast and cache-efficient
```

---

## Route Structure Rules

Follow the locked route blueprint.

Approved route groups:

```txt
/
tattoo-styles/
tattoo-services/
gallery/
reviews/
tattoo-blog/
faq/
contact/
```

Do not invent new SEO route systems casually.

---

## SEO Rendering Rules

SEO-critical content must remain:
- server-rendered
- crawlable
- visible without JS dependency

Avoid:
- client-only metadata
- client-rendered schema
- hidden SEO content

---

## Image Rules

Use:
- `next/image`
- responsive sizing
- lazy loading
- optimized formats

Avoid:
- raw `<img>` tags
- oversized media payloads
- loading huge galleries immediately

---

## Font Rules

Use:
- `next/font`

Avoid:
- external blocking font loading
- layout-shifting typography

---

## Middleware Rules

Use middleware minimally and intentionally.

Allowed:
- region/domain detection
- lightweight redirects

Avoid:
- heavy logic in middleware
- unnecessary request overhead

---

## Environment Rules

Environment variables must remain:
- centralized
- secure
- server-safe

Never expose secrets to client bundles.

Only expose browser-safe values using:
```txt
NEXT_PUBLIC_
```

---

## Performance Rules

Prioritize:
- low hydration cost
- optimized bundles
- server rendering
- fast LCP
- minimal client JS

Avoid:
- unnecessary providers
- global client wrappers
- oversized client state systems

---

## Accessibility Rules

Maintain:
- semantic layouts
- proper heading hierarchy
- keyboard-safe navigation
- reduced motion support

---

## Forbidden Patterns

Never introduce:

- Pages Router
- `getServerSideProps`
- `getStaticProps`
- client-rendered SEO pages
- global `"use client"` abuse
- unnecessary runtime rendering
- duplicated metadata systems
- inline secret usage
- unstable route architecture

---

## Final Principle

Every App Router decision should reinforce:

- SEO safety
- performance
- server-first rendering
- scalability
- stability
- maintainability
- architectural consistency

If an implementation weakens those goals, do not implement it.